{
 "paper_id": "10.3389_fpsyg.2026.1855838",
 "info": {
  "title": "Influence of social exclusion on aggressive behavior among specialized school students: the mediating roles of relative deprivation and deviant peer affiliation",
  "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1855838",
  "file_name": "10.3389_fpsyg.2026.1855838.pdf",
  "keywords": [
   "aggressive behavior",
   "deviant peer affiliation",
   "relative deprivation",
   "social exclusion",
   "specialized school students"
  ],
  "abstract": "Objective: This study examined the association between social exclusion and aggressive behavior among students in specialized schools and investigated the mediating roles of relative deprivation and deviant peer affiliation. Method: A total of 491 students from specialized schools completed the Ostracism Experience Scale for Adolescents, Relative Deprivation Questionnaire, Deviant Peer Affiliation Questionnaire, and Buss–Perry Aggression Questionnaire. Results: Social exclusion was significantly and positively associated with aggressive behavior among students in specialized schools. Relative deprivation and deviant peer affiliation independently mediated the association between social exclusion and aggressive behavior. In addition, relative deprivation and deviant peer affiliation formed a significant serial mediating pathway between social exclusion and aggressive behavior. Conclusion: Social exclusion was directly associated with aggressive behavior and was also indirectly associated with aggressive behavior through relative deprivation and deviant peer affiliation. These findings provide practical implications for the prevention and intervention of aggressive behavior among students in specialized schools."
 },
